  "worked_solution": "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $5$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(A+4I)^{5}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A+4I)^{4}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ must be a $15\\times 15$ matrix.\n\n- Since the rank of $(A+4I)^{4}$ equals $8,$ then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A+4I)^{4}=15-8=7.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A+4I)^{4}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A+4I)^{4}$ of dimension $7$ in ${R}^{15}.$\n\n- Since the rank of $(A+4I)^{5}$ equals $6,$  then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A+4I)^{5}=15-6=9.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A+4I)^{5}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A+4I)^{5}$ of dimension $9$ in ${R}^{15}.$\n\nSince $\\text{Null}(A+4I)^{4}$ is a subspace of dimension $7$ in $\\text{Null}(A+4I)^{5}$, which is a subspace of dimension $9,$ our basis can contain only $2$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(A+4I)^{5}$ but do not lie in $\\text{Null}(A+4I)^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A+4I)^{5}-\\text{nullity}(A+4I)^{4}=9-7=2\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $5$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4.$",
